        <div id="terminology" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does Gas Generator Mean Gasoline or Natural Gas?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually it means gasoline. When people shop for a gas generator, they almost always mean a portable machine with its own tank that you fill from a can. Natural gas generators do exist, but nearly all of them are permanently installed standby units piped into the home's gas line. The two are separate purchases, with different prices, installation and safety considerations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a gasoline portable trade away?</h3>

          <p>
            This guide is about gasoline portables. They cost a fraction of what a standby unit does, need no installation beyond a transfer switch or interlock, and can be rolled to wherever you need power. The trade is that you handle the fuel, the starting, the connection and the placement yourself in every outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do natural gas standby units fit?</h3>

          <p>
            If you would rather have a machine that starts by itself and runs for days off a utility gas line, our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac cost guide</a> breaks down what a natural gas standby system really costs once installed, and our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator rankings</a> set standby, gasoline, dual fuel and battery options side by side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is there a middle ground?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Dual fuel portables run on gasoline or propane, and tri-fuel models add natural gas through a quick-connect. They give up a little output on the gaseous fuels in exchange for far easier long-term fuel storage, and they deserve a look if the fuel headaches described below worry you. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual fuel generator guide</a> ranks them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="house-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Gas Generator Do You Need for a House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A house on city water with gas heat and no basement typically needs 2,000 to 3,500 running watts, while a house with a private well usually lands in the 8,000-watt class or above. The well pump, the sump and any air conditioning decide which side of that line you fall on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you build a load list?</h3>

          <p>
            List what has to run, not what would be nice to have. For most homes that is the refrigerator, freezer, lights, internet, a gas furnace blower in winter, a sump pump if there is a basement, and a well pump if the house is not on city water. Read each appliance's running watts from its data plate, add them up, then add the single biggest starting surge on top.
          </p>

