          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is a 240V well pump the dividing line?</h3>

          <p>
            A 240-volt well pump needs a generator with a 120/240V outlet, usually an L14-30 or a 14-50, connected through a transfer switch or interlock. Its starting surge can run two to three times its running draw. Together those two facts push most houses with a well into the 8,000-watt class or higher. Our <a href="/best-generator-for-well-pump/">well pump generator guide</a> covers that case in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much headroom should you leave?</h3>

          <p>
            Plan to use no more than about 75 to 80 percent of the running rating. A generator held at 90 percent of its rating for days on end runs hot, loud and thirsty, and the voltage sags that come with it are hard on motors. If your list adds up to 6,000 watts, an 8,000-watt unit is the right size; a 6,500-watt unit is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can a gas generator be too big?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. A 9,500-watt generator feeding 2,000 watts of load burns almost as much fuel per hour as it does at half load. When your essentials are modest, a mid-size inverter or a smaller open frame runs far longer on the same can of gasoline. For homes where 4,500 watts is plenty, our <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">small generator picks</a> are the better starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-burn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Gas Does a Generator Use per Day?
          </h2>

          <p>
            An 8,000-watt open frame burning about 0.7 gallons an hour uses roughly 17 gallons in a day of nonstop running, and three days takes more than 50 gallons. Smaller inverters use a fraction of that, and running in cycles can cut any of these totals by half or more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Gasoline Consumption</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.1-0.3 gal/hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">2,200W inverter, light load</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.3-0.5 gal/hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">4,500W inverter, moderate load</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.6-0.8 gal/hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">8,000W open frame, half load</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.8-1.2 gal/hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">9,500W+ open frame, heavy load</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why do fuel plans fall apart?</h3>

          <p>
            Fuel is where most gas generator plans break down. Makers list run time at 25 or 50 percent load, which is fair, but owners routinely underestimate how many hours they will run and overestimate how easy fuel will be to buy during a regional outage. Gas stations need electricity to pump, and the few that have it draw long lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            The consumption figures above are typical ranges, not specifications, so check the manual for your model. At around 0.7 gallons an hour, three days of round-the-clock running needs more gasoline than most people can store safely or legally at home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does cycling cut fuel use?</h3>

          <p>
            Run in cycles. A refrigerator and freezer hold their cold for hours once chilled, a sump pit can usually be pumped down and left for a while in most storms, and a house with gas heat loses warmth slowly. Two hours on followed by three or four hours off can cut fuel use by half or more. Many owners pair this routine with a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">battery power station</a> that carries the router, phones and a few lights while the generator rests.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you do the math before buying?</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply your realistic running hours per day by the gallons per hour at your expected load, then multiply by the number of days your typical long outage lasts. If the answer is uncomfortably large, pick an inverter, pick dual fuel, or plan to cycle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storing-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Store Gasoline for a Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Store it stabilized, in approved sealed cans, outside the living space, and rotate it every few months. Pump gasoline was never meant for long-term storage, and treating it like an emergency supply that keeps forever is how generators end up refusing to start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does gasoline go bad?</h3>

          <p>
            Its lighter compounds evaporate, it oxidizes into gums and varnish, and the ethanol in most blends pulls moisture out of the air. Left untreated, it can start causing small-engine starting problems within a month or two, and after several months it can leave deposits that only a carburetor cleaning will clear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you use fuel stabilizer?</h3>

          <p>
            Add stabilizer every time you fill a storage can, not only before the generator is put away for winter. Labels commonly claim one to two years of protection, but rotation is still the better habit: pour stored gasoline into your car every few months and refill the cans with fresh, stabilized fuel. Where you can buy ethanol-free gasoline, it stores better and is gentler on small engines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should the cans live?</h3>

          <p>
            Use approved, tightly sealed containers, preferably with spill-proof spouts, and keep them out of the living space: a detached shed or garage, away from water heaters, furnaces, dryers and anything else with a flame or spark, and out of direct sun. Local fire codes often cap how much gasoline you can keep at home, commonly at around 25 gallons in a residential garage, so look up your local rule before you stockpile. Set the cans on a shelf or floor away from where children play, and label each one with the date it was filled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you refuel safely?</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the engine off and let it cool for several minutes before adding fuel. Gasoline spilled on a hot muffler or exhaust is one of the most common ways generators start fires.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">STORAGE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stabilize every can, rotate every season.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Think of stored gasoline as milk with a long date: fine for a while, a little worse every month. Add stabilizer when you fill the can, write the date on it, burn it in your car within a few months, and refill. The generator starts when you need it, and you never have to pour out a can of varnish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Gasoline Better Than Propane or Natural Gas for a Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gasoline gives the most power per gallon and full rated output, but it is the hardest of the three to store. Propane keeps for years without breaking down and comes in standard cylinders sold almost everywhere. Natural gas never needs storing, but it only works through a fixed connection, usually on a standby unit.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fuel</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shelf life</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Output vs gasoline</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main drawback</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gasoline</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Months, longer with stabilizer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full rated output</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Goes stale; hard to buy in outages</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indefinite in a sound tank</td>
