                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Natural gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No storage needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 10 to 20 percent less</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Requires a gas line and fixed installation</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is propane the backup worth planning around?</h3>

          <p>
            For anyone running a gasoline portable, propane is the most useful second fuel to consider. A dual fuel generator lets you burn stabilized gasoline first and then switch to a row of 20-pound cylinders once the cans are empty. Propane also ends the stale-fuel carburetor problem, because the carburetor holds no liquid fuel while running on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does gasoline-only still make sense?</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline remains the right call for many households. It has the highest energy density of the three, you can rotate it through the family car, and gasoline-only units tend to be a little cheaper and simpler. The condition is that fuel planning becomes part of the purchase rather than an afterthought.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="frame-vs-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Should You Buy an Open-Frame or Inverter Gas Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy an open frame if you need 240 volts for a well pump, a large sump or a window air conditioner, and an inverter if your loads are a refrigerator, furnace blower and lights. The inverter can use half the gasoline over an outage; the open frame brings the surge and voltage bigger loads demand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does an open-frame generator work?</h3>

          <p>
            Open-frame generators hold a fixed engine speed to produce 60-hertz power directly. They cost less per watt and come in large sizes with 240V outlets, which is why they dominate the 8,000-watt-and-up class. They are also louder, and they burn fuel at a fairly steady rate regardless of load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does an inverter do differently?</h3>

          <p>
            Inverter generators change engine speed to match demand and build clean 60-hertz power electronically. They are quieter, much more efficient at light loads, and safer for electronics. Most are 120V only and stop at around 4,500 watts, though a handful of larger inverter models exist at higher prices. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">inverter generator guide</a> compares them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which one saves more gasoline?</h3>

          <p>
            In a household where the generator mostly runs a refrigerator, furnace blower and lights, an inverter can burn half the gasoline an open frame would over the same outage. In a house with a well pump, a larger sump pump or a window air conditioner, the open frame's 240V output and surge capacity are what the job requires.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is there a practical compromise?</h3>

          <p>
            For some homes, yes: own a mid-size inverter for ordinary outages and borrow or rent more capacity for the rare extreme event, or pair an open frame with a battery station so the engine only runs part of the day. Either approach shrinks the amount of fuel you need to keep on hand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Gas Generator Features Are Worth Paying For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Automatic carbon monoxide shutoff comes first, then electric start and a 120/240V outlet that matches your inlet. After those, an hour meter, a fuel gauge, solid wheels and the right emissions certification are worth checking before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Automatic CO shutoff</h3>

          <p>
            Make <strong>automatic CO shutoff</strong> the first feature you insist on. It adds little to the price, it is now common across the major brands, and it targets the way generators actually kill people. Look for ANSI/PGMA G300 or UL 2201 on the box or the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric start</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Electric start</strong> matters more than it seems in the store. Yanking a recoil cord on a 450cc engine in freezing rain is hard work, and for older owners it can decide whether the generator gets used at all. Remote start fobs are a pleasant extra, but they do not change the placement rules: the generator still sits 20 feet or more from the house. Keep the pull cord as a backup and keep the starting battery charged.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The right outlet, meter, gauge and wheels</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>A 120/240V outlet that matches your inlet</strong> is essential for a house with a well pump or a transfer switch. An <strong>hour meter</strong> makes oil-change intervals easy to keep track of, and a <strong>fuel gauge</strong> saves you from opening a hot tank to check the level. Never-flat tires and a folding handle earn their keep on anything heavier than about 150 pounds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Emissions certification and warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Check the emissions certification as well. California and some other states allow only CARB-compliant small engines to be sold, and most national brands now ship CARB versions; EPA certification is standard. Finally, compare warranties. Three years of residential coverage is common among the major brands, and a local service dealer is worth more than an extra year printed on paper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="house-connection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Connect a Gas Generator to Your House Safely?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Through a <strong>manual transfer switch</strong> or a listed <strong>panel interlock kit</strong>, each fed by an outdoor power inlet and installed by a licensed electrician with a permit. Those are the only safe and legal ways to run hardwired loads such as the furnace, the well pump or a sump pump on its own circuit, which is where a large gasoline portable earns its keep. Never backfeed the house without one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BACKFEED WARNING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No dryer-outlet shortcuts, ever.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Plugging a generator into a dryer or wall receptacle with a double-male cord sends power backward through your panel and onto the utility lines, where transformers step it up to thousands of volts and put line workers and neighbors at risk. It bypasses the protection built into your home, can wreck the generator when grid power returns, violates the National Electrical Code and is illegal in most jurisdictions.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which inlet matches your generator?</h3>

          <p>
            Size the inlet to the generator's largest outlet. Most portables from 7,000 to 9,500 watts carry an L14-30 30-amp 120/240V locking outlet, and many bigger units add a 14-50 50-amp outlet. A 30-amp connection delivers up to about 7,200 watts; if your generator can supply more and your load list needs it, a 50-amp inlet puts the extra capacity to use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a transfer switch or interlock cost?</h3>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch, usually covering six to ten selected circuits, commonly runs $800 to $1,800 installed, including the inlet and permit. An interlock kit is often cheaper, roughly $500 to $1,200 installed, and lets you pick any circuit, but you have to manage the load yourself so the generator is not overloaded. Either costs far less than a fire or an injury. Our <a href="/best-generator-transfer-switch/">transfer switch guide</a> compares the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What cord and GFCI issues come up?</h3>

          <p>
            Run a properly rated generator cord from the unit to the inlet, as short as the 20-foot-plus placement rule allows, and route it so nobody trips over it. Some newer generators have GFCI protection that can trip when connected to a house panel because of the way neutral and ground are bonded; your electrician can resolve that correctly. A circuit breaker finder speeds up labeling the circuits you want to include.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Where Should a Gas Generator Sit to Avoid Carbon Monoxide?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows, vents and your neighbors' homes. Gasoline generators produce large amounts of carbon monoxide, an odorless, colorless gas that kills people every storm season, often while they sleep, and most of those deaths involve generators run in garages, basements or right beside the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should it never run?</h3>

          <p>
            Never in a garage, even with the door open. Never in a basement, crawlspace, carport or shed, and never on a roofed porch next to the house. Open doors and fans do not make an enclosed space safe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which alarms and shutoffs help?</h3>

          <p>
            Install CO alarms that keep working without grid power, either battery-only or with battery backup, on every level and near the bedrooms, and test them before storm season. Choose a generator with automatic CO shutoff, sold under names such as COsense, CO-MINDER and CO Shield and certified to ANSI/PGMA G300 or UL 2201; it kills the engine when carbon monoxide builds up around it. Treat it as a safety net, never as permission to move the generator closer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you run it in rain or snow?</h3>

          <p>
            Use a generator tent or ventilated cover made for use while the engine runs, or a freestanding canopy out in the open. Weather is never a reason to bring the generator into a garage or under the eaves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are the warning signs?</h3>

          <p>
            A headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion in anyone in the house, or a sounding CO alarm, means everyone goes outside into fresh air immediately. Call 911 once you are out.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE 20-FOOT RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Outside, 20 feet out, exhaust aimed away.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No garage, basement, carport, shed or covered porch, whatever the weather. Battery-backed CO alarms inside on every level, and a CO shutoff on the generator as a second layer, never a first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="startable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. How Do You Keep a Gas Generator Ready to Start?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run it monthly under load, store it the way the manual says, and keep the oil and starting battery fresh. Most gas generators that let their owners down in an outage fail at the pull cord or the key switch, not under load, and the cause is almost always stale fuel, a dead battery or old oil.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run it monthly with a real load</h3>

          <p>
            Once a month, start the generator and let it run for 15 to 30 minutes with something plugged in, such as a space heater or a shop light. That pulls fresh fuel through the carburetor, charges the starting battery on models that do so, and exposes problems while there is still time to fix them calmly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick a storage approach and stick to it</h3>

          <p>
            Either keep the tank full of stabilized fuel, which limits air space and condensation, or close the fuel valve and run the carburetor dry before storing the machine. Manufacturers disagree about which is better, so follow your manual. What you should never do is leave untreated gasoline sitting in the carburetor for months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Change the oil on schedule and keep the battery charged</h3>

          <p>
            A new engine needs an early first oil change, often after a few hours of break-in, and then regular changes based on run hours. During a long outage, check the oil every day, because a multi-day run can reach the service interval quickly. On electric-start units, keep the battery on a maintainer if the generator does not charge it between uses, and replace it every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes With Gas Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on buying fuel during the outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In a regional outage, gas stations close, and the few that can still pump draw long lines. Owners who plan to buy fuel as they go often run dry by day two. Keep enough stabilized gasoline for your realistic first two or three days, rotate it, and cycle the generator so every gallon goes further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the biggest generator on the shelf</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 12,000-watt generator running only a fridge and lights burns gasoline nearly as fast as it would at half load, and it is far harder to move and store. Size to your real load list plus about 20 to 25 percent headroom. For modest loads, a mid-size inverter does the same job on much less fuel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refueling a hot engine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pouring gasoline into a running or just-stopped generator risks splashing it onto a hot muffler, where it can ignite. Switch the engine off, give it several minutes to cool, refuel carefully with a spill-proof spout, wipe up any drips, and only then restart. Keep a fire extinguisher rated for flammable liquids within reach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it with fuel that goes stale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fresh gasoline in your tank today is stale gasoline a few months from now. A generator left through the off-season with untreated fuel often refuses to start in the first storm. Add stabilizer to every can when you fill it, run the unit under load once a month, and follow the manual's long-term storage steps.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some owners reach the panel by plugging a generator into a dryer receptacle with a homemade double-male cord. That pushes power onto the utility lines, where it can kill line workers, and it can start fires and wreck the generator when grid power returns. Have an electrician install a transfer switch or interlock with an inlet instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many gallons of gas does a generator use in a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That comes down to size and load. Over 24 hours, a 2,200-watt inverter at light load may burn 2 to 4 gallons, a 4,500-watt inverter roughly 6 to 10, and an 8,000-watt open frame at half load about 14 to 19. Cycling the generator, a couple of hours on and a few hours off, can cut those totals by half or more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can gasoline sit in a generator before it goes bad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Untreated gasoline, especially an ethanol blend, can cause small-engine starting trouble within a month or two. Stabilizer added while the fuel is still fresh stretches that, and labels commonly claim one to two years. The safer habit is to rotate stored fuel every few months, run the generator once a month, and follow the owner's manual for storage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a gas generator all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, provided it sits outdoors at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust aimed away, has enough fuel, and battery-powered CO alarms are working inside. Never refuel it while it runs. Many owners shut it down overnight once the fridge and freezer are cold, or hand overnight loads to a battery station, which saves fuel and keeps neighbors happy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is gasoline or dual fuel better for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For outage backup, dual fuel usually comes out ahead, because propane keeps indefinitely and gives you a second fuel when gasoline runs short. Expect roughly 10 percent less output on propane. Gasoline-only models are simpler and sometimes cheaper. If you will reliably rotate stabilized gasoline, either type works; if you will not, dual fuel is more forgiving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas generator runs a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                True whole-house coverage, including central air, an electric water heater or electric cooking, usually takes 15,000 to 25,000 watts or more, which is standby territory. A large portable rated at 9,500 to 12,000 running watts can carry most of a gas-heated house through a transfer switch, especially when you manage loads. Households that only need essentials need far less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much gasoline can I legally store at home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Local fire codes set the limit, and it varies. Many residential codes allow around 25 gallons in approved containers in a garage or shed, while some places are stricter or ban storage in attached garages. Ask your local fire department, and keep fuel out of the living space and away from ignition sources, in approved, sealed cans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas generators need a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not when you only run extension cords from the generator to individual appliances. Powering hardwired loads such as a furnace, well pump or panel circuits requires a manual transfer switch or a listed interlock kit with an inlet, installed by a licensed electrician under a permit. Plugging the generator into a wall or dryer outlet instead is dangerous and illegal.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

